    "narrative": "With the 2026 season reaching its crescendo, **Jonathan Aranda** serves as the focal point of today\u2019s offensive strategy. His score of 57.57 reflects a calculated upside play against May; while not elite, his promotion to the #2 spot in the order, combined with a favorable platoon advantage against a right-handed arm, elevates his ceiling sufficiently to justify the start. We are aggressively monitoring the rest of the lineup as official cards are submitted. **Justin Crawford** and **Masyn Winn** remain our primary focal points for the SB category, and if their respective matchups against high-walk-rate pitchers hold, they will anchor our pursuit of the stolen base threshold, which remains our highest-priority category for this stretch run.\n\nThe bench decisions are currently dictated by the league-wide schedule, as the bulk of our core\u2014including **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Bryce Harper**, **Cal Raleigh**, and **Lawrence Butler**\u2014is sidelined due to a league-wide off-day. **Gleyber Torres**, **Matt Chapman**, and **Daulton Varsho** are being held in reserve to manage our remaining game caps, as their current daily efficiency scores failed to eclipse the 60.0 threshold required to burn a cap in this specific matchup climate. We are keeping a close eye on **Jung Hoo Lee** and **Zack Gelof**; if late-breaking lineup data suggests a shift in hitting conditions or defensive alignment, one of them could be rotated in to replace the marginal value currently projected from the bottom of the order.\n\nRisks today are centralized around the volatility of the mid-rotation arms we are facing. While **Jonathan Aranda** benefits from a shaky 3.99 xERA matchup, we must remain vigilant regarding weather shifts in the East Coast parks that could stifle the power output of **Gavin Sheets** and **JJ Bleday**. Furthermore, should **Yainer Diaz** or **Will Smith** be held out of the starting lineup to rest catchers, our depth will be severely tested, 